# Defect 273, 2026-10-04. `read_file` of a directory died `panic: out of
# memory`, exit 134, on Linux arm64 (Debian clang 22.1.8), where its type
# promises `.err`; this Mac answered `read_failed`, and a file under `/proc`
# read on Linux as an empty text at exit 0. The runtime sized the file by
# `fseek(SEEK_END)` and `ftell`, which answer for a regular file only: a
# directory's end there is an offset near 2^63. The open file is now asked what
# it is, a directory fails before a byte is read, and every read goes on to the
# file's end. `build/` is a directory wherever the compiler runs, so the case
# reads it on every platform; the path below it is one nothing writes.

function main()
    match read_file("build")
        .ok text => print("read a directory: ", len(text), " bytes")
        .err e   => print("a directory: ", e.code)

    match read_file("build/heroes-golden-273-absent.txt")
        .ok _  => print("read a file that is not there")
        .err e => print("absent: ", e.code)
